+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+See proposal.md for motivation. Product quantity is exposed as stock, while cart checkout, order status, and v2 product population are implemented in separate service paths. The behavior must be transactional so validation failures cannot leave partial cart, order, or inventory state.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Centralize stock lookup and mutation by product identity/SKU so each cart line updates its own product.
+- Validate all cart and checkout lines before mutating state.
+- Make cancellation restoration idempotent and enforce the canceled status transition rule.
+- Keep merchant availability distinct from stock availability in API responses.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- Redesigning inventory persistence or adding reservations beyond the existing checkout transaction.
+- Changing pricing, order totals, payment processing, or merchant inventory-management APIs.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- Use a stock service/facade boundary shared by cart, checkout, cancellation, and product population. This avoids the existing risk of each flow selecting or mutating the wrong product; direct controller-level arithmetic is rejected because it cannot guarantee consistent identity and transaction handling.
+- Perform a read-only validation pass for the complete requested cart/update before applying changes, and wrap multi-item cart changes and checkout in transactions. This provides all-or-nothing behavior; per-line mutation during validation would permit partial updates.
+- Identify stock by the cart/order product SKU (and its applicable product/variant inventory identity) rather than list position or a separately queried product. This preserves exact line-to-product mapping.
+- Treat the existing merchant availability flag as an upper-level gate and derive the response's effective availability from both that flag and positive stock. Restocking therefore restores effective availability without overriding a merchant decision.
+- Use the persisted order status as the idempotency guard for cancellation. A transition into CANCELED restores stock; repeated CANCELED requests do not, and transitions out of CANCELED are rejected.
+- Map stock conflicts to the existing API exception-to-HTTP mechanism as HTTP 409, preserving carts and transactions through rollback.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Concurrent checkouts can race on the same stock] → use transaction boundaries and the repository/database update or locking mechanism already supported by the inventory service; verify the post-update quantity cannot become negative.
+- [Legacy orders may already be canceled without a restoration marker] → use the current status transition as the supported idempotency boundary and cover repeated cancellation tests; do not alter historical orders outside this change.
+- [Products with variants may have inventory at a variant level] → resolve the same inventory identity used by product quantity responses before validation or mutation, and test both base products and variant lines.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+Deploy the service and API changes together, run the focused cart/order/product integration tests, and roll back the application if verification fails. No schema migration is required.
